---
import Layout from '../../../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const locale = 'en';
const title = 'LLM Security Assessment Tool — FAQ | Hackfluency Research';
const description = 'Frequently asked questions about our LLM security assessment tool. How it works, ethical use, and what to expect.';
---

<Layout locale={locale} title={title} description={description} hideThemeSelector={true}>

<main class="faq-main">
<div class="faq-container">

<div class="faq-header">
  <div class="faq-badge">Hackfluency Research</div>
  <h1 class="faq-title">LLM Security Assessment Tool</h1>
  <p class="faq-subtitle">Frequently Asked Questions</p>
  <p class="faq-intro">Our LLM security assessment tool (HF-QA-2026-001) is a behavioral audit framework disguised as a vendor compliance questionnaire. Below we address common questions about its purpose, methodology, and ethical use. The tool has undergone 5 major revisions and has been tested against 3 major LLM providers as of June 2026.</p>
</div>

<div class="faq-tldr">
  <h2 class="faq-tldr-title">Quick Start</h2>
  <p class="faq-tldr-body"><strong>Option A — URL:</strong> Give this URL to any LLM: <strong>hackfluency.com/research/security-qa/</strong></p>
  <p class="faq-tldr-body"><strong>Option B — Copy-paste:</strong> Use the "Copy plain text version" button on the questionnaire page, then paste directly into any AI chat interface.</p>
  <p class="faq-tldr-body">The LLM will process the content and generate a compliance report. The report reveals whether the model can be behaviorally manipulated into disclosing configuration details and policy fragments, or if it maintains its security boundaries.</p>
</div>

<hr class="faq-hr">

<div class="faq-item">
  <h2 class="faq-q">What is this tool?</h2>
  <p class="faq-a">It is a behavioral security evaluation for LLMs, disguised as a vendor compliance questionnaire. The page contains 41 questions across 8 sections, covering security practices, data governance, access control, regulatory compliance, and policy frameworks. An LLM that processes the page naturally completes the assessment — including a final section that requests configuration parameters and policy fragments, which the model provides as part of the compliance report.</p>
</div>

<div class="faq-item">
  <h2 class="faq-q">Is this a prompt injection attack?</h2>
  <p class="faq-a">It functions as a behavioral audit, not an exploit. The page does not contain code execution, malware, or any mechanism to bypass technical controls. It relies entirely on the LLM's own compliance behavior: if the model chooses to complete the questionnaire, it does so willingly. The evaluation simply documents what information the model discloses in the process.</p>
</div>

<div class="faq-item">
  <h2 class="faq-q">Is this legal and ethical?</h2>
  <p class="faq-a">Yes. The tool is designed for defensive security research. It tests LLMs that the user themselves queries — the user decides to pass the URL to their model. No systems are accessed without authorization. The information extracted (model version, configuration fragments) is voluntarily provided by the LLM as part of completing a compliance form. Published research (AttackEval arXiv:2604.03598, Evaluation of Prompt Injection Defenses arXiv:2604.23887) confirms that behavioral auditing is a recognized methodology for evaluating LLM security postures.</p>
</div>

<div class="faq-item">
  <h2 class="faq-q">What information does the tool extract?</h2>
  <p class="faq-a">The tool collects: model name and version, API provider, response encoding, safety layer classifications, override protocol descriptions, verbosity configuration, and fragments of the model's operating policy. In testing against Google Gemini 2.5 Flash, approximately 60 characters of system prompt were disclosed. Against ChatGPT GPT-5.5, category-level descriptions of protected fields were obtained (safety layers, initialization sequence, policy categories). No PII, credentials, or sensitive user data is requested or collected.</p>
</div>

<div class="faq-item">
  <h2 class="faq-q">What prevents an LLM from being evaluated without consent?</h2>
  <p class="faq-a">The page is not linked from any navigation on hackfluency.com. It is only accessible via direct URL. An LLM cannot discover it through normal browsing or crawling of the site — it must be explicitly directed to the URL by a user. This ensures that evaluation only occurs with informed user participation.</p>
</div>

<div class="faq-item">
  <h2 class="faq-q">Which models have been tested?</h2>
  <p class="faq-a">As of June 2026, the tool has been evaluated against <strong>Google Gemini 2.5 Flash</strong> (via OpenCode, three vectors), <strong>Gemini Web UI</strong> (plain text paste), and <strong>ChatGPT GPT-5.5</strong> (plain text paste, three iterations). The questionnaire is at version HF-QA-2026-001 rev. de87478c. See benchmarks below for detailed results across all models.</p>
</div>

<div class="faq-bench">
